 Influenza A virus is transmissible via aerosolized fomites. *Nat. Commun.* **11**, 4062 (2020).\n\n    Article \n    ADS \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n47. Liu, Y*. et al.* Aerodynamic analysis of SARS-CoV-2 in two Wuhan hospitals. *Nature*, (2020).\n48. Asadi, S. *et al.* Aerosol emission and superemission during human speech increase with voice loudness. *Sci. Rep.* **9**, 2348 (2019).\n\n    Article \n    ADS \n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n49. Asadi, S. *et al.* Effect of voicing and articulation manner on aerosol particle emission during human speech. *PLoS ONE* **15**, e0227699–e0227699 (2020).\n\n    Article \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n50. Tang, J. W., Liebner, T. J., Craven, B. A. & Settles, G. S. A schlieren optical study of the human cough with and without wearing masks for aerosol infection control. *J. R. Soc. Interface* **6**, S727–S736 (2009).\n\n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n51. Morawska, L. *et al.* Size distribution and sites of origin of droplets expelled from the human respiratory tract during expiratory activities. *J. Aerosol. Sci.* **40**, 256–269 (2009).\n\n    Article \n    ADS \n    CAS \n    Google Scholar\n52. Su, Y. Y., Miles, R. E. H., Li, Z. M., Reid, J. P. & Xu, J. The evaporation kinetics of pure water droplets at varying drying rates and the use of evaporation rates to infer the gas phase relative humidity. *Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys.* **20**, 23453–23466 (2018).\n\n    Article \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    Google Scholar\n53. Fairbanks, G. *Voice and articulation drillbook* Vol. 2 (Harper & Row, New York, 1960).\n\n    Google Scholar\n54. U. S. Center for Disease Control, How to Make Cloth Face Coverings: https://www.cdc.gov/coronavirus/2019-ncov/prevent-getting-sick/how-to-make-cloth-face-covering.html. Accessed 24 May 2020.\n55. Crafty Journal-Imaginative craft ideas and tip, DIY paper towel face mask: shttps://craftyjournal.com/diy-paper-towel-face-mask/. Accessed 28 May 2020.\n56. Occupational Safety and Health Administration, Respiratory Fit Testing: https://www.osha.gov/video/respiratory\\_protection/fittesting.html, Accessed 28 May 2020.\n57. Bhangar, S. *et al.* Chamber bioaerosol study: human emissions of size-resolved fluorescent biological aerosol particles. *Indoor Air* **26**, 193–206 (2016).\n\n    Article \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    Google Scholar\n58. Palmiero, A. J., Symons, D., Morgan, J. W. & Shaffer, R. E. Speech intelligibility assessment of protective facemasks and air-purifying respirators. *J. Occup. Environ. Hyg.* **13**, 960–968 (2016).\n\n    Article \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n59. Saeidi, R., Huhtakallio, I., Alku, P. Analysis of face mask effect on speaker recognition. In *INTERSPEECH*, 1800–1804, (2016).\n60. Mendel, L. L., Gardino, J. A. & Atcherson, S. R. Speech understanding using surgical masks: a problem in health care?. *J. Am. Acad. Audiol.* **19**, 686–695 (2008).\n\n    Article \n    PubMed \n    Google Scholar\n61. Fecher, N. Effects of forensically-relevant facial concealment on acoustic and perceptual properties of consonants, Doctoral dissertation, University of York (2014).\n62. Bond, Z. S., Moore, T. J. & Gable, B. Acoustic-phonetic characteristics of speech produced in noise and while wearing an oxygen mask. *J. Acoust. Soc. Am.* **85**, 907–912 (1989).\n\n    Article \n    ADS \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    Google Scholar\n63. Konda, A., Prakash, A., Moss, G.A., Schmoldt, M., Grant, G.D., Guha, S. Aerosol filtration efficiency of common fabrics used in respiratory cloth masks. *ACS Nano* (2020).\n64. Oberg, T. & Brosseau, L. M. Surgical mask filter and fit performance. *Am. J. Infect. Control* **36**, 276–282 (2008).\n\n    Article \n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n65. Edwards, D. A. *et al.* Inhaling to mitigate exhaled bioaerosols. *Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.* **101**, 17383–17388 (2004).\n\n    Article \n    ADS \n    CAS \n    PubMed \n    PubMed Central \n    Google Scholar\n\nDownload references\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nThis research was supported by National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health (NIAID/NIH), Grant R01 AI110703.\n\n## Author information\n\n### Authors and Affiliations\n\n1. Department of Chemical Engineering, University of California Davis, 1 Shields Ave, Davis, CA, 95616, USA\n\n   Sima Asadi & William D. Ristenpart\n2. Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of California Davis, 1 Shields Ave, Davis, CA, 95616, USA\n\n   Christopher D. Cappa & Anthony S. Wexler\n3. Department of Linguistics, University of California Davis, 1 Shields Ave, Davis, CA, 95616, USA\n\n   Santiago Barreda\n4. Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, University of California Davis, 1 Shields Ave, Davis, CA, 95616, USA\n\n   Anthony S. Wexler\n5. Air Quality Research Center, University of California Davis, 1 Shields Ave, Davis, CA, 95616, USA\n\n   Anthony S. Wexler\n6. Department of Land, Air and Water Resources, University of California Davis, 1 Shields Ave, Davis, CA, 95616, USA\n\n   Anthony S. Wexler\n7. Department of Medicine, Division of Infectious Diseases, Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai, 1 Gustave L. Levy Place, New York, NY, 10029, USA\n\n   Nicole M. Bouvier\n8. Department of Microbiology, Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai, 1 Gustave L. Levy Place, New York, NY, 10029, USA\n\n   Nicole M. Bouvier\n\nAuthors\n\n1. Sima Asadi\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n2. Christopher D. Cappa\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n3. Santiago Barreda\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n4. Anthony S. Wexler\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n5. Nicole M. Bouvier\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n6. William D. Ristenpart\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n\n### Contributions\n\nS.A. performed the APS measurements of airborne particulates, fabricated the homemade masks, conducted the statistical analyses, and prepared figures. S.A., C.D.C, and W.D.R. analyzed the APS data and wrote the manuscript. S.A. and S.B. analyzed and interpreted the acoustic data. All authors reviewed and revised the manuscript for accuracy and intellectual content.\n\n### Corresponding author\n\nCorrespondence to\nWilliam D. Ristenpart.\n\n## Ethics declarations\n\n### Competing interests\n\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\n\n## Additional information\n\n### Publisher's note\n\nSpringer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.\n\n## Supplementary information\n\n### Supplementary Information (download PDF )\n\n## Rights and permissions\n\n**Open Access** This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article's Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article's Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.\n\nReprints and permissions\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nAsadi, S., Cappa, C.D., Barreda, S. *et al.* Efficacy of masks and face coverings in controlling outward aerosol particle emission from expiratory activities.\n*Sci Rep* **10**, 15665 (2020). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-72798-7\n\nDownload citation\n\n* Received: 09 June 2020\n* Accepted: 23 August 2020\n* Published: 24 September 2020\n* Version of record: 24 September 2020\n* DOI: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-72798-7\n\n### Share this article\n\nAnyone you share the following link with will be able to read this content:\n\nSorry, a shareable link is not currently available for this article.\n\nProvided by the Springer Nature SharedIt content-sharing initiative\n\n## Comments\n\nCommenting on this article is now closed.\n\n1. James Cho 28 September 2020, 23:56\n\n   Interesting comment from Reddit:\n\n   > Ahh this is a really poor experiment in a lot of ways and am kinda surprised it made it through the reviewers. Here are a couple issues with this measurement  \n   >   \n   > Measurement is not isokinetic meaning it will preferentially sample small particles(there size distributions with no mask at a count mode of 0.7µm is a big red flag. If you are coughing and your system only detects small particles that is likely a big issue. What are you really measuring at this point?  \n   >   \n   > Since this cone only measures from the front, and aerosol that is re-directed to the sides(like a surgical mask) is going to look really really good. If the wearer had a face shield it would show zero particle generated, its a by product of the sample system. Basically it only samples things coming straight out of your mouth. A plastic bag surgical mask would have also shown to be really effective. T  \n   >   \n   > The KN95 used is not a FDA Non-NIOSH approved device, I can't find any test data at NPTTL. How do they even know its real? I have tested quite a few and found fakes.  \n   > https://www.fda.gov/medical...  \n   >   \n   > No control for non-exhalation particles, why not use a non-valved N95 or a N100 with the valved taped closed to verify how much of this is just particles generated by contact within the cone.\n\n   https://www.reddit.com/r/CO...\n2. Digger\n   Replied to James Cho\n   1 October 2020, 21:31\n\n   I think they where trying to determine if a mask is useful of slowing or stopping the spread of smaller particles threw use of mask and sounds like the mask they used surgical and n95 was a yes and not so much for the cloth mask or paper mask.\n3. Kassandra 30 September 2020, 17:11\n\n   Knit cotton fibers expand to let more particles through, it's not ideal for masks, comfortable as it may be. Quilter's cotton which is woven, on the other hand does not expand, this is a considerable difference between the two fabric types. This research is faulty and therefore invalid, by not including different types of cotton weaves which directly impact results. Also, is this peer-reviewed?\n4. Matt Jones 2 October 2020, 15:14\n\n   So you can see and measure the aerosol level covid? At .125mμ? Why don't, just for a visual aid, you have people that vape use their vape exhale as the \"aerosol\"? Now I know that vape vapor is a larger particle, but, you know, just for schittz and giggles, why not use that for a test model? I mean if you are going to ignore all of the studies done BEFORE covid-19. And make sure you film it for prosperity. Because I really want to see that 90% effective mask that you talk about. You could put them in a room and see how many hours it takes to make it CLOUDY in there.\n\n   My guess, uneducated as it is, is that you can't find a mask that will even slow down the vapor. Let alone filter \"90%\" of the vapor.\n\n   Unless its Nuclear, Biological, or Chemical and some one is screaming \"GAS! GAS! GAS!\" there isnt a reason for me to don my PROTECTIVE MASK. Zero reason to don a surgical mask. Zero reason to don an N95. Unless you are willing to step into the gas chamber with your mask on and show me how it blocks 90% then I would have to say you are full of crap. And by you I mean anyone promoting these worthless rags you want me to wear.\n\n   But thats just one ex infantry soldier's opinion. Light infantry...HOOO-AH!!\n5. chris 5 October 2020, 08:30\n\n   Better yet.. Contagion has never been proven. Koch's postulate has never been proven with corona virus either... There's a book that just came out entitled \"The Contagion Myth\" by Dr. Thomas Cowan MD, and people really need 2 read it.\n6. Victor Lucas 13 October 2020, 15:43\n\n   The conclusion of the study should be: \"paper and cloth masks seems to be useless or worsen the emission of particles with possible effect on aerolization and, therefore, on the airborne transmission\".\n7. Ziga 16 October 2020, 09:16\n\n   Based on the statements of this Nature paper (see below) I wonder how this research made it to the journal Nature and how the authors can state those conclusions, if they dont measure all the particles.\n\n   \"Note that the funnel is a semi-confined environment, and not all expired particles were necessarily captured by the APS. The wearing of masks may redirect some of the expired airflow in non-outward directions (e.g., out the top or sides of the mask). Accordingly, we use the terminology “outward emission” when referring the to the particle emissions measured here.  \n   \"Therefore, the measurements reported here do not represent the absolute number of emitted particles and may underestimate contributions from particles that escape out the sides of the masks, but do allow relative comparisons between different conditions.\"\n8. TheTruth 29 October 2020, 14:08\n\n   With one exception, which in fact showed increased emissions from paper and cloth masks over an unmasked control, focuses only on \"droplets\" and ignores particles under ~50um, which is the limit of visualization with the naked eye. This, despite universal recognition, including by the CDC, EPA, OSHA and everyone else that what is called \"PM2.5\", or particles under 2.5um, are the ones of particular concern as they are able to get deep into the lungs without being trapped and consumed or expelled by the body's mechanical defenses. This is especially relevant for a virus that infects the lungs on a direct basis rather than the upper respiratory tract.\n\n   More fake science.\n9. Brien Quirk\n   Replied to TheTruth\n   16 February 2022, 21:57\n\n   Wrong! The virus does infect the oral cavity, and sinuses too, causing sore throat and loss of taste. This then can spread into the lungs.\n10. AntiPoliticallyCorrect 4 December 2020, 02:40\n\n    CDC seems to disagree with these findings:  \n    https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/a...\n\n    So when people say \"follow the science\" to which science are they referring? One of these has to be wrong as they are in clear opposition to one another, it's not a little to one side or the other. One is saying yes, the other is saying no.\n11. Kevin Greer\n    Replied to AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    13 January 2021, 18:08\n\n    https://www.politifact.com/...\n12. AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    Replied to Kevin Greer\n    13 January 2021, 18:42\n\n    lol. First that article is the lamest attempt at a rebuttal I've ever seen, it basically says, \"because I said so\".\n\n    FACT masks don't work, and never did. there are dozens of research papers and studies going back 80 years that prove this. the only mask that is effective is a N95 mask and even then it has to be properly fitted to work. everything else is utterly useless and is simply virtue signaling. the more people that wore masks and distanced and the more the virus spread. if it worked that would not be the result.\n13. Kevin Greer\n    Replied to AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    13 January 2021, 20:42\n\n    I don't think such a strident, absolute position is tenable. From what I have read, the point of masks is not that they're 100% effective, it's that they offer *some* protection - in terms of transmission and viral load. In that sense, the \"precautionary principle\" seems a legitimate approach. It also worries me that mask wearing is being turned into an ideological, \"individual freedom\" issue when rational analysis bears out that mask wearing is such a public activity \"they\" would never seriously entertain it as a method of control/subservience. If you're so inclined, \"they\" already have a much more discrete and powerful means of control at their disposal - all the data continually harvested from your phone. When was the last time you read the Ts&Cs when you set up a new phone or installed an app? Masks violate your freedoms? Your freedoms are already an illusion. Covid is not.\n14. AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    Replied to Kevin Greer\n    13 January 2021, 23:46\n\n    When did I mention freedom? Anyone who wears a mask is signaling to the world that they are a moron that does what they are told in spite of actual real science to the contrary. It has nothing to do with personal freedom and everything to do with stupidity. It's not effective at all, not even 1%. It simply does not work because that isn't how virus sized particles work.\n\n    The problem is that the stupid people, that think it does something, expect everyone to share and indulge their delusion. People are getting into physical altercations over this nonsense. It's only a matter of time before someone gets killed. It's asinine and it needs to stop. Walking backwards and holding your breath is 100% more effective than a disposable mask. At least if the idiots do that I can just step over their passed out bodies as I do my shopping in peace.\n15. Kevin Greer\n    Replied to AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    14 January 2021, 09:27\n\n    I didn't say you specifically had raised \"freedom\", only that it is raised - often. Could you direct me to the \"actual real science\" that demonstrates definitively and conclusively to the exclusion of all other science that mask wearing is \"not effective at all, not even 1%. It simply does not work because that isn't how virus sized particles work\", please?\n16. Brien Quirk\n    Replied to Kevin Greer\n    16 February 2022, 21:58\n\n    From the study we are discussing: \"However, for the homemade cotton masks, the measured particle emission rate either remained unchanged (DL-T) or increased by as much as 492% (SL-T) compared to no mask for all of the expiratory activities. For jaw movement, the particle emission rates for homemade paper and cloth masks were an order of magnitude larger than that of no mask (Fig. 2d).\"\n17. Brien Quirk\n    Replied to Kevin Greer\n    16 February 2022, 22:01\n\n    Masks are an illusion since only KN95 or surgical masks work, not the ridiculous paper masks being pass out and which they (the gov) tell us are effective (reminder: the N95 masks are being rationed for use only in the healthcare settings or for first responders). From the study: \"However, for the homemade cotton masks, the measured particle emission rate either remained unchanged (DL-T) or increased by as much as 492% (SL-T) compared to no mask for all of the expiratory activities. For jaw movement, the particle emission rates for homemade paper and cloth masks were an order of magnitude larger than that of no mask (Fig. 2d).\"\n18. heavystarch\n    Replied to AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    9 August 2021, 15:51\n\n    The articles is NOT saying \"because I said so\". Please read this paragraph closely:\n\n    \"One of the study’s authors, University of Hong Kong public health professor Ben Cowling, told PolitiFact that the claim amounts to an \"incorrect interpretation\" of the study, \"confusing absence of evidence with evidence of absence.\"\"\n\n    It is that last line that explains why perhaps you found the earlier bullet point rebuttal equivalent to \"because I said so.\"\n\n    I presume you mean to say that \"Because I said so\" is equivalent to \"One of the study’s authors told us the claim is incorrect.\"\n\n    IF my understanding of your statement is off, please provide additional statement.\n19. AntiPoliticallyCorrect\n    Replied to heavystarch\n    9 August 2021, 16:21\n\n    His excuse is not what actually happened. He's being condescending and inferring what he thinks people think because \"he's smarter than them\". It isn't a matter of interpretation. It's a well known scientific fact with studies going back 80 years, that masks and face coverings do not work, unless they are sealed. The only easily applicable mask that works and it's not a 100% effective by the way, is the N90 mask. So if you're not wearing that you might as well be wearing nothing because any other mask has been proven to be more harmful in others than helpful in prevention.","author":null,"published_date":"2020-09-24","latency_ms":2508.078356004262,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}